  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a fundamentally different methodology that combines: nonlinear programming with coordinate descent, adaptive filtering based on FFT analysis, and targeted optimization of the high-frequency tails. The core idea is to:\n\n- Decompose the optimization into alternating coarse and fine adjustments\n- Use FFT to identify the most constraining frequency bands\n- Apply scaled coordinate updates based on local gradient information\n- Maintain feasibility through exponential transformation\n- Accelerate convergence via asynchronous gradient estimates\n\nThis method differs structurally from previous approaches by completely separating the optimization controller and gradient policy, enabling adaptive constraint handling that can theoretically outperform existing local methods.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe optimization problem: Min_{a \u2208 R^n} AC1(a) subject to 0 \u2264 a_i \u2264 1000 \u2200i\n\nParametrization transformation: Let b_i = log(a_i / 10) for improved convexity properties\n\nObjective decomposition: AC1(a) = (2n \u2225a\u2297a\u2225\u221e) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\nConvolution representation: (a\u2297a)[k] = \u2211_{i+j=k} a[i]a[j]\n\nGradient computation: \n\u2202AC1/\u2202a_i = 2/||a||\u00b2 \u03a3_k [\u03b4_{k,k_max} * (a[k-k_max] + a[k+k_max-i])]\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n**Phase 1: Initialization**\n- Load parent sequence and normalize sum to 1.0\n- Compute spectral profile via FFT\n- Identify dominant frequency bands\n\n**Phase 2: Coordinate Optimization**\n- For each dimension i:\n  Compute local gradient g_i = sum_j a[j]|j-i|\n  Select adaptive step size \u03bb = 0.1 * exp(-\u0394_i)\n  Update candidate = exp(\u03bb * (g_i)) / sum(exp(\u03bb * g))\n\n**Phase 3: Asynchronous Refinement**\n- Maintain parallel active set estimates\n- Merge gradient approximations\n- Apply dual-projection constraints\n\n|| Strategy continues in full response ||",
